explanation:
This exercise uses a Map in one process. Map.has(key) checks presence; get reads the record; set stores it.

Keep the stored response separate from the object returned to a caller. Return a fresh shallow copy of the result on both a new request and a retry, so a caller changing a top-level returned field cannot overwrite the saved response. Object spread, {...savedResult}, copies top-level fields; it would still share nested objects, so this exercise uses flat result fields.

```javascript
const saved = new Map();
saved.set("req-a", {status:201});
console.log(saved.has("req-a"));
console.log(saved.get("req-a").status);
console.log(saved.has("req-b"));
console.log(saved.get("req-b"));
```
The outputs are true, 201, false, undefined. set receives a key/value, stores the entry and returns the Map; has returns presence; get returns the value or undefined. Reading a stored object does not copy it, so the response-copy rule above still matters.

